course: dictionary
lesson: 8705
title: Context clues: senate
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: You can often work out a hard word from the words around it. These are its context, and they leave clues. «The Senate was a council of experienced, mostly rich men who advised the leaders and controlled money and foreign affairs.» Council, advised and leaders are the clues, so a senate is a group of important people who give advice and help rule.
explain: A capital S, as in the Senate, is a name for one particular group. «Both have two parts: the US Congress has the House of Representatives and the Senate.» The clue is the word parts: the Senate is one of two parts of a group that makes laws.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try "group" in place of senate: «The Roman group of important men advised the leaders.» It still makes sense, so your guess is close. Then check a dictionary to make it exact.
fact: A senate is one of the two parts of the group that makes laws in some countries.
fact: The USA, Australia, Canada and Ireland (where it is called the Seanad) have a Senate, and it is often called the «upper house».
fact: In ancient Rome, the Senate was a council of important men who advised and ruled the Roman Republic.
fact: In the UK, the matching part of Parliament is the House of Lords, not a Senate.
fact: In some universities, the senate is the group of senior teachers who run the university.
exercise: «The Senate was a council of experienced men who advised the leaders.» What is a senate in this sentence? | A small shop selling bread | A loud shout of joy | *A group of important men who give advice | A fast type of boat
exercise: What is the Senate in the USA? | A kind of sports team | The part that cooks the food for the president | The part that mends the roads | *One of the two parts of the group that makes laws
exercise: «Both have two parts: the US Congress has the House of Representatives and the Senate.» Which two parts are named? | the Senate and a school | *the House of Representatives and the Senate | the Senate and the Roman Republic | the House of Commons and the Senate of Rome
test: «A US bill must pass the House and the Senate and then be signed by the president.» Why must a bill pass both? | The president can ignore the House | Only the Senate can make a law alone | *Both parts must agree before it can become a law | A bill is a type of boat
test: In ancient Rome, what was the Senate? | *A council of important men who advised and ruled | A king's palace by the river | A shop that sold togas | A school for young children
exam: In some universities, what is the senate? | *A group of senior teachers who run the university | A library full of books | A student club | A sports team
